window.docContents[244].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___7___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Representation of a Lie algebra\u0009Representation of a Lie algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___7___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Characters-of-irreducible-representations-form-a-basis-of-class-functions/#Chapter4___Theorem4___2___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.2. Characters\u0009Characters of irreducible representations form a basis of class functions\u0009Characters of irreducible representations form a basis of class functions","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___2___-Characters/Characters-of-irreducible-representations-form-a-basis-of-class-functions/#Chapter4___Theorem4___2___1___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_3/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S3":{"contents":"(2) The symmetric group S_3. In the symmetric group S_n, conjugacy classes are determined by cycle decomposition sizes: two permutations are conjugate if and only if they have the same number of cycles of each length. For S_3, there are three conjugacy classes, so there are three different irreducible representations over \\mathbb{C}. If their dimensions are d_1, d_2, d_3, then d_1^2 + d_2^2 + d_3^2 = 6, so S_3 must have two 1-dimensional and one 2-dimensional representations. The 1-dimensional representations are the trivial representation \\mathbb{C}_+ given by \\rho(\\sigma) = 1 and the sign representation \\mathbb{C}_- given by \\rho(\\sigma) = (-1)^\\sigma.\n\nThe 2-dimensional representation can be visualized as representing the symmetries of the equilateral triangle with vertices 1, 2, 3 at the points (\\cos 120°, \\sin 120°), (\\cos 240°, \\sin 240°), (1, 0) of the coordinate plane, respectively. Thus, for example,\n\n\\rho((12)) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\rho((123)) = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos 120° & -\\sin 120° \\\\ \\sin 120° & \\cos 120° \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nTo show that this representation is irreducible, consider any subrepresentation V. The space V must be the span of a subset of the eigenvectors of \\rho((12)), which are the nonzero multiples of (1, 0) and (0, 1). Also, V must be the span of a subset of the eigenvectors of \\rho((123)), which are different vectors. Thus, V must be either \\mathbb{C}^2 or 0.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Irreducible representations of S_3","header":"Irreducible representations of S_3","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_3/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___6___-Representations-of-products--Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j--Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary result under the displayed hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.10.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule stable under the displayed tensor-factor actions is bottom or top.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.10.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.10.2; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary representation of a product group admits the displayed tensor-product description.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.6.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.6. Representations of products\u0009Irreducible representations of G x H are tensor products V_i ⊗ W_j\u0009Irreducible representations of G x H are tensor products V_i ⊗ W_j\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___6___-Representations-of-products/Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___6___-Representations-of-products--Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j--Irreducible-representations-of-G-x-H-are-tensor-products-V_i-___-W_j--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules--Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A module is projective exactly when it is a retract of a suitable module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Theorem8.1.1; role=supporting]\n\nA module is projective exactly when applying linear maps from it preserves the displayed short exact sequence conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Theorem8.1.1; role=supporting]\n\nA module is projective exactly when every surjective linear map onto it admits a linear right\ninverse.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Theorem8.1.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Equivalent characterizations of projective modules\u0009Equivalent characterizations of projective modules\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules--Equivalent-characterizations-of-projective-modules--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});